{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1952 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "745c45e9343cc566d0b6be4f8bb81a42650e496d46c0238aea3ccf1e2ad467c8" :edb/db-before-t 1951 :edb/db-after-t 1952 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535264 50 #inst "2026-10-13T11:11:52.169Z" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186063937 1071 "Solve the equation $4x = y$ for the variable $x$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186063937 1071 "Solve the equation $4x = y$ for the variable $x.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186063937 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $x$. To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nIn this case, we simply divide both sides of the equation by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\require{cancel} 4x &= y \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {4x} {4} &= \\dfrac y 4 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {{\\cancel{4}}x} {\\cancel{4}} &= \\dfrac y 4 \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac y4 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186063937 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $x.$ To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nIn this case, we simply divide both sides of the equation by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\t4x &= y \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {4x} {4} &= \\dfrac y 4 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {{\\cancel{4}}x} {\\cancel{4}} &= \\dfrac y 4 \\[5pt]\n\t\tx &= \\dfrac y4\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186063938 1071 "Solve the following equation for the variable $x$:\n\n$\\dfrac{3x-2y}{3}=\\dfrac{1+2y}{4}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186063938 1071 "Solve the following equation for the variable $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x-2y}{3}=\\dfrac{1+2y}{4}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186063938 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $x$. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x - 2y}{3} &= \\frac{1 + 2y}{4} \\\\\n4 \\cdot (3x - 2y) &= 3 \\cdot (1 + 2y) \\\\\n12x - 8y &= 3 + 6y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $8y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x - 8y &= 3 + 6y \\\\\n12x - 8y + 8y &= 3 + 6y + 8y \\\\\n12x &= 3 + 14y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x &= 3 + 14y \\\\\n\\frac{12x}{12} &= \\frac{3 + 14y}{12} \\\\\n\\frac{12x}{12} &= \\frac{3 + 14y}{12} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3 + 14y}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186063938 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $x.$ First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-2y}{3} & =\\frac{1+2y}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot (3x-2y) & =3\\cdot (1+2y) \\\\ 12x-8y & =3+6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $8y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-8y & =3+6y \\\\ 12x-8y+8y & =3+6y+8y \\\\ 12x & =3+14y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $12\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x & =3+14y \\\\ \\frac{12x}{12} & =\\frac{3+14y}{12} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}x}{\\cancel{12}} & =\\frac{3+14y}{12} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3+14y}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186063950 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac{x}{3} + 2y = 1$, express $x$ in terms of $y$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186063950 1071 "Given that $\\dfrac{x}{3} + 2y = 1,$ express $x$ in terms of $y.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186063950 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $x$. To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $2y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{3} + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x}{3} + 2y - 2y &= 1 - 2y \\\\\n\\frac{x}{3} + 0 &= 1 - 2y \\\\\n\\frac{x}{3} &= 1 - 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{3} &= 1 - 2y \\\\\n3 \\cdot \\frac{x}{3} &= 3 \\cdot (1 - 2y) \\\\\n3 \\cdot \\frac{x}{3} &= 3 \\cdot (1 - 2y) \\\\\nx &= 3 \\cdot (1 - 2y) \\\\\nx &= 3 \\cdot 1 - 3 \\cdot 2y \\\\\nx &= 3 - 6y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186063950 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $x.$ To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $2y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3}+2y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}+2y-2y & =1-2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}+0 & =1-2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =1-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & =1-2y \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{x}{3} & =3\\cdot (1-2y) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{3}} & =3\\cdot (1-2y) \\\\ x & =3\\cdot (1-2y) \\\\ x & =3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2y \\\\ x & =3-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186064347 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488192 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186064347 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488254 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186064348 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488194 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186064348 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488239 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186064349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488243 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186064349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488244 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186064445 1050 540.0 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186065414 1064 "Equations can contain more than one variable. For example, the following equation has two variables, $\\color{blue}x$ and ${\\color{red}y}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x} - 7{\\color{red}y} = 1\n$$\n\nTo **solve the equation for the variable ** ${\\color{blue}{x}},$ we need to isolate $\\color{blue}x$ on one side of the equation:  \n\nIn this case, we apply the addition principle and add $7{\\color{red}y}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-7y & =1 \\\\ x-7y+7y & =1+7y \\\\ x+0 & =1+7y \\\\ x & =1+7y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully isolated the variable ${\\color{blue}{x}}.$\n\nLet's now look at an example where we must apply the multiplication principle." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186065414 1064 "Equations can contain more than one variable. For example, the following equation has two variables, $\\color{blue}x$ and ${\\color{red}y}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x} - 7{\\color{red}y} = 1\n$$\n\nTo **solve the equation for the variable** ${\\color{blue}{x}}$, we need to isolate $\\color{blue}x$ on one side of the equation:\n\nIn this case, we apply the addition principle and add $7{\\color{red}y}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 7y &= 1 \\\\\nx - 7y + 7y &= 1 + 7y \\\\\nx + 0 &= 1 + 7y \\\\\nx &= 1 + 7y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully isolated the variable ${\\color{blue}{x}}$.\n\nLet's now look at an example where we must apply the multiplication principle." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186066202 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487926 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186066202 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487926 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068495 1071 "What is the solution to the equation $6^x - 6^5= 0$?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186068495 1071 "What is the solution to the equation $6^x - 6^5= 0?$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068495 1074 "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $6^5$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6^{x} - 6^{5} &= 0 \\\\\n6^{x} - 6^{5} + 6^{5} &= 0 + 6^{5} \\\\\n6^{x} - 6^{5} + 6^{5} &= 0 + 6^{5} \\\\\n6^{x} &= 6^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we can equate the exponents.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6^{x} &= 6^{5} \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x} = {\\color{blue}5}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186068495 1074 "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $6^5$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{x}-{6}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {6}^{x}-{6}^{5}+{6}^{5} & =0+{6}^{5} \\\\ {6}^{x}-\\cancel{{6}^{5}}+\\cancel{{6}^{5}} & =0+{6}^{5} \\\\ {6}^{x} & ={6}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation share the same base $(6),$ we can equate the exponents. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{x} & ={6}^{5} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${\\color{blue}x} = {\\color{blue}5}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068496 1071 "What is the solution to $3^x = \\dfrac{1}{9}$?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186068496 1071 "What is the solution to $3^x = \\dfrac{1}{9}?$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068496 1074 "First, we rewrite $9$ as $3^2$ and then proceed as before:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{x} &= \\frac{1}{9} \\\\\n3^{x} &= \\frac{1}{3^{2}} \\\\\n3^{x} &= 3^{-2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and get $x=-2$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186068496 1074 "First, we rewrite $9$ as $3^2$ and then proceed as before:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {3}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\ {3}^{x} & ={3}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation share the same base $(3),$ we equate the exponents and get $x=-2.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068497 1071 "Solve the equation $2\\cdot 3^x=54$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186068497 1071 "Solve the equation $2\\cdot 3^x=54.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068497 1074 "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot 3^{x} &= 54 \\\\\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3^{x}}{2} &= \\frac{54}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3^{x}}{2} &= 27 \\\\\n3^{x} &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite $27$ as $3^3$ so that both sides have the same base:\n\n$$\n3^{x} = 3^{3}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}3}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186068497 1074 "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{x} & =54 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {3}^{x}}{2} & =\\frac{54}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}}{\\cancel{2}} & =27 \\\\ {3}^{x} & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite $27$ as $3^3$ so that both sides have the same base:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x}={3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents, we get ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}3}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488215 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488218 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488241 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488279 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488260 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488284 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488240 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186068938 1050 420.0 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186069158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186069158 1071 "If $2 \\cdot 6^{k} = 72$, then $k =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186069158 1071 "If $2\\cdot {6}^{k}=72,$ then $k=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186069158 1074 "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by dividing both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {6}^{k} & =72 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {6}^{k}}{2} & =\\frac{72}{2} \\\\ {6}^{k} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $36$ as ${6}^{2}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{k} & =36 \\\\ {6}^{k} & ={6}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{k} & ={6}^{2} \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $k=2.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186070028 1064 "What is the value of $x$ that satisfies the following equation?\n\n$$\n3^x = 3^4\n$$\n\nWe call this an **exponential equation** because the variable is contained within the exponent.\n\nNow, this equation is pretty easy to solve. Because the bases on both sides of the equation are the same (they are both equal to $3$), we can simply equate the powers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{x} &= 3^{4} \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to this equation is $x=4$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186070028 1064 "What is the value of $x$ that satisfies the following equation?\n\n$$\n3^x = 3^4\n$$\n\nWe call this an **exponential equation** because the variable is contained within the exponent. \n\nNow, this equation is pretty easy to solve. Because the bases on both sides of the equation are the same (they are both equal to $3$), we can simply equate the powers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & ={3}^{4} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to this equation is $x=4.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186070029 1064 "Let's take a look at the following equation:\n\n$$\n5^x = 25\n$$\n\nWe can't solve this equation straight off the bat because the two sides of the equation don't share a common base.\n\nHowever, notice that $25$ can be written as a power of $5$:\n\n$$\n25=5^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n5^x = 5^2\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are now written as an exponential expression with a common base of $5$, we can set the exponents equal to each other and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{x} &= 5^{2} \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check that the solution is correct, we plug $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 5^{2} &= 25 \\\\ 25 &= 25 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186070029 1064 "Let's take a look at the following equation: \n\n$$\n5^x = 25\n$$\n\nWe can't solve this equation straight off the bat because the two sides of the equation don't share a common base.\n\nHowever, notice that $25$ can be written as a power of $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n25=5^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n5^x = 5^2.\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation are now written as an exponential expression with a common base of $5,$ we can set the exponents equal to each other and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check that the solution is correct, we plug $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t5^{2} &= 25 \\\n\t\t25 &= 25 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071277 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071278 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071279 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071280 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186071281 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487678 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487729 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487946 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487960 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186071276 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487989 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186071276 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487946 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186073507 1071 "![](images/1a/1afba9a36c6fd04fecfd50877181380776e13a83422697de4a39d71381f09aff.png)\n\n \n\nFind the value of $x$ in the triangle above." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186073507 1071 "![](images/1a/1afba9a36c6fd04fecfd50877181380776e13a83422697de4a39d71381f09aff.png)\n\nFind the value of $x$ in the triangle above." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186073507 1074 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles in any triangle is equal to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n36^{∘} + 112^{∘} + x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n148^{∘} + x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 32^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186073507 1074 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles in any triangle is equal to $180^\\circ$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {36}^{∘}+{112}^{∘}+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={32}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186073508 1071 "![](images/e4/e43daa39b169c8ba29c21dd33e7a7fab1e92c7c78eafbd1021971e1883f6c9f8.png)\n\n \n\nFind the measure of $\\angle C.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186073508 1071 "![](images/e4/e43daa39b169c8ba29c21dd33e7a7fab1e92c7c78eafbd1021971e1883f6c9f8.png)\n\nFind the measure of $\\angle C$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186073508 1074 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3z + 4z + 40^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n7z + 40^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n7z &= 140^{∘} \\\\\nz &= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠C &= 4z \\\\\n&= 4(20^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186073508 1074 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+4z+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7z+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7z & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & =4z \\\\  & =4({20}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186073509 1071 "Consider a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, where $m\\angle{A} = 55^\\circ$, $m\\angle B = x + 10^\\circ$, and $m\\angle C = 3x - 5^\\circ$. Find the value of $x$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186073509 1071 "Consider a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, where $m\\angle{A} = 55^\\circ$, $m\\angle B = x + 10^\\circ$, and $m\\angle C = 3x - 5^\\circ.$ Find the value of $x.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186073509 1074 "The sum of the measures of the interior angles must be $180^\\circ$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n55^{∘} + (x + 10^{∘}) + (3x - 5^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n60^{∘} + 4x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n4x &= 120^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186073509 1074 "The sum of the measures of the interior angles must be $180^\\circ.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{55}^{∘}+(x+{10}^{∘})+(3x-{5}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {60}^{∘}+4x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={120}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186073510 1071 "![](images/1a/1a24187f280eb99640c3d1bfae3b7fe873383f04c2c492e7873a40de16a9560b.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $C$ given that $m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{B}$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186073510 1071 "![](images/1a/1a24187f280eb99640c3d1bfae3b7fe873383f04c2c492e7873a40de16a9560b.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $C$ given that $m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{B}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186073510 1074 "Using\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A} = m\\angle{B}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= m∠B \\\\\n3x - 27^{∘} &= 2x \\\\\nx &= 27^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, substituting $x=27^\\circ$ in the expression for $m\\angle B$, we get\n\n$$\nm\\angle{B}=2(27^\\circ)=54^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, we can find $m\\angle C$ using the fact that the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 54^{∘} + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n108^{∘} + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠C &= 72^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186073510 1074 "Using $m\\angl