x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =-12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+x(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3Q+2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P=2,Q=1.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139535264 175921