egin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{90}^{∘}+{46}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={180}^{∘}-{136}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={44}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠B$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $∠A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC<AB<AC\n$$\n\nand therefore, the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC},\\overset{―}{AB},\\overset{―}{AC}.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186116446 1064 "Triangles can be classified by sides or by angles.\n\nFirst, let's consider the classifications by sides. We have three groups of triangles:\n\n- **Equilateral** triangles, where all sides have the same length.\n\n- **Isosceles** triangles, where only two sides are congruent. The congruent sides are called the **legs** of the triangle, and the third side is called the **base**.\n\n- **Scalene** triangles, where all sides have different measures.\n\n![](images/dc/dc38034d6c202b5168fc8184511a839eb41af206eb733e71e5f5c2fa970c0e33.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186116446 1064 "Triangles can be classified by sides or by angles.\n\nFirst, let's consider the classifications by sides. We have three groups of triangles:\n\n- **Equilateral** triangles, where all sides have the same length.\n- **Isosceles** triangles, where only two sides are congruent. The congruent sides are called the **legs** of the triangle, and the third side is called the **base**.\n- **Scalene** triangles, where all sides have different measures.\n\n![](images/dc/dc38034d6c202b5168fc8184511a839eb41af206eb733e71e5f5c2fa970c0e33.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186116447 1064 "Now, let's consider classifying triangles by angles. We have three groups of triangles.\n\n- In an **acute** triangle, all three angles are acute.\n\n- A triangle with a right angle is called a **right** triangle.\n\n- A triangle with an obtuse angle is called an **obtuse** triangle.\n\n![](images/f1/f1c28d1f4b1ec7a93f1778e63be3727aa227e8eea5320eb7d5579c697e709429.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186116447 1064 "Now, let's consider classifying triangles by angles. We have three groups of triangles.\n\n- In an **acute** triangle, all three angles are acute.\n- A triangle with a right angle is called a **right** triangle.\n- A triangle with an obtuse angle is called an **obtuse** triangle.\n\n![](images/f1/f1c28d1f4b1ec7a93f1778e63be3727aa227e8eea5320eb7d5579c697e709429.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186116448 1064 "Consider the following scalene triangle, where $AB=5$, $BC=7$, and $AC=6$ units, respectively.\n\n![](images/57/57be69075640f742ffe6c004515485c4f479347f3b8b523272778948935048df.png)\n\nLooking at the angles, it's hard to tell which angle is the largest.\n\nHowever, since we know the sides, there is a simple rule that we can use to determine the relative size of the angles:\n\n> Longer sides are opposite to larger angles and vice versa.\n\nApplying this rule to $\\triangle{ABC}$ depicted above, the longest side is $\\overline{BC}$. Therefore, the largest angle is the angle opposite to $\\overline{BC}$, which is $\\color{red}\\angle{A}$.\n\n![](images/82/82d9ac6d61f285f3557d2dd0dda826803ffae306c5f9a927a422b3b953f262f4.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- the mid-sized side ($\\overline{AC}$) is opposite to the mid-sized angle ($\\angle B$), and\n- the shortest side ($\\overline{AB}$) is opposite to the smallest angle ($\\angle C$).\n\nSo, from least to greatest, the angles of the triangle are ordered as follows:\n$m\\angle{C} < m\\angle{B} < m\\angle{A}$\n\n**Note:** This rule works in reverse too. If you know the biggest angle of a triangle, the longest side is always opposite to this angle." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186116448 1064 "Consider the following scalene triangle, where $AB=5$, $BC=7,$ and $AC=6$ units, respectively.\n\n![](images/57/57be69075640f742ffe6c004515485c4f479347f3b8b523272778948935048df.png)\n\nLooking at the angles, it's hard to tell which angle is the largest.\n\nHowever, since we know the sides, there is a simple rule that we can use to determine the relative size of the angles:\n\nLonger sides are opposite to larger angles and vice versa.\n\nApplying this rule to $\\triangle{ABC}$ depicted above, the longest side is $\\overline{BC}.$ Therefore, the largest angle is the angle opposite to $\\overline{BC},$ which is $\\color{red}\\angle{A}.$\n\n![](images/82/82d9ac6d61f285f3557d2dd0dda826803ffae306c5f9a927a422b3b953f262f4.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- the mid-sized side ($\\overline{AC}$) is opposite to the mid-sized angle ($\\angle B$), and\n\n- the shortest side ($\\overline{AB}$) is opposite to the smallest angle ($\\angle C$).\n\nSo, from least to greatest, the angles of the triangle are ordered as follows:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{C} < m\\angle{B} < m\\angle{A}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This rule works in reverse too. If you know the biggest angle of a triangle, the longest side is always opposite to this angle." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186124503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488228 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186124526 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488203 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186124555 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488263 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186124572 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488186 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186124572 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488262 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186124572 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488273 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186124572 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488274 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186124605 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488246 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186125026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122756 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186125026 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186125026 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186125048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122888 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186129189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488188 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186129189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488189 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186129190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488199 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186129674 1074 "![](images/ea/ea36f1ec1ddeb03dd8d133fb8d65b5d50b3fa07ba34495978d87f95742dee042.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-x\\,⟹\\,x=4-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e82098a4bb63f1d709a06b6a24da078594716d6a3cc0fa2e069af9dcd9154955.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186129674 1074 "![](images/ea/ea36f1ec1ddeb03dd8d133fb8d65b5d50b3fa07ba34495978d87f95742dee042.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-x\\,⟹\\,x=4-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e82098a4bb63f1d709a06b6a24da078594716d6a3cc0fa2e069af9dcd9154955.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186129697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127663 13194139535264 1