ue] [17592186167154 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼Po(5)$ given below, compute $P(X > 4)$.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.1247$ | $0.2650$ | $0.4405$ | $0.6160$ | $0.7622$ | $0.8666$ | $0.9319$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186167154 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(5)$ given below, compute $P(X>4).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.1247\\,$ | $\\,0.2650\\,$ | $\\,0.4405\\,$ | $\\,0.6160\\,$ | $\\,0.7622\\,$ | $\\,0.8666\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186167154 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-F(4) \\\\  & =1-0.4405 \\\\  & =0.5595.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186167155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186167155 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼Po(2)$ given below, compute $P(X \\ge 4)$.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.6767$ | $0.8571$ | $0.9473$ | $0.9834$ | $0.9955$ | $0.9989$ | $0.9998$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186167155 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(2)$ given below, compute $P(X\\ge 4).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.6767\\,$ | $\\,0.8571\\,$ | $\\,0.9473\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9955\\,$ | $\\,0.9989\\,$ | $\\,0.9998\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186167155 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nNow, note that \n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=P(X>3).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =P(X>3) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-F(3) \\\\  & =1-0.8571 \\\\  & =0.1429.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186168157 1064 "When performing certain types of statistical tests (e.g., hypothesis testing), we often need to use standard cumulative distribution function (CDF) tables to carry out the test.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to construct CDF tables for the Poisson distribution and use them to calculate probabilities.\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of the Poisson distribution $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is a positive parameter.\n\nThe CDF of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's compute the values of $F(x)$ for the distribution $\\textrm{Po}(2)$ at $x=0$ and $x=1$.\n\nTo do that, we use the definition of the cumulative distribution function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(0) &= P(X \\le 0) \\\\\n&= P(X = 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{0} \\cdot e^{-2}}{0!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot e^{-2}}{1} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1353 \\\\\nF(1) &= P(X \\le 1) \\\\\n&= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{0} \\cdot e^{-2}}{0!} + \\frac{2^{1} \\cdot e^{-2}}{1!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot e^{-2}}{1} + \\frac{2 \\cdot e^{-2}}{1} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.4060\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe CDF data is usually presented as a table. If we were to continue this process and calculate $F(2), F(3), F(4)$, and $F(5)$, our table would look as follows:\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.1353\\,$ | $\\,0.4060\\,$ | $\\,0.6767\\,$ | $\\,0.8571\\,$ | $\\,0.9473\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\cdots\\,\\,\\,$ |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $F(x)$ is an increasing function.\n- Since $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ takes arbitrarily large values, we have $F(x) < 1$ for all $n$, and $F(x) \\to 1$ as $x\\to\\infty$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186168157 1064 "When performing certain types of statistical tests (e.g., hypothesis testing), we often need to use standard cumulative distribution function (CDF) tables to carry out the test.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to construct CDF tables for the Poisson distribution and use them to calculate probabilities.\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of the Poisson distribution $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is a positive parameter.\n\nThe CDF of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's compute the values of $F(x)$ for the distribution $\\textrm{Po}(2)$ at $x=0$ and $x=1.$  \n\nTo do that, we use the definition of the cumulative distribution function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =P(X\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(X=0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.1353 \\\\ F(1) & =P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{0}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{0!}+\\frac{{2}^{1}\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1}+\\frac{2\\cdot {e}^{-2}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.4060\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe CDF data is usually presented as a table. If we were to continue this process and calculate $F(2), F(3), F(4),$ and $F(5),$ our table would look as follows:\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.1353\\,$ | $\\,0.4060\\,$ | $\\,0.6767\\,$ | $\\,0.8571\\,$ | $\\,0.9473\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\cdots\\,\\,\\,$ |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $F(x)$ is an increasing function.\n\n- Since $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ takes arbitrarily large values, we have $F(x) < 1$ for all $n,$ and $F(x) \\to 1$ as $x\\to\\infty.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186168609 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168613 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186171897 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n- $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: y-x\\geq 0\\}$\n\n- $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\}$\n\n- $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: x^2+y^2> 4\\}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186171897 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: y-x\\geq 0\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: x^2+y^2> 4\\}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488195 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488200 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488201 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172050 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488245 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488202 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488220 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488270 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488225 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172179 1050 420.0 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172291 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n1. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2} \\mid x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1}$\n2. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2} \\mid (x, y) \\ne (1, 1)}$\n3. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2} \\mid 2x - y \\ne 0}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186172291 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (1,1)\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,2x-y\\ne 0\\}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186172291 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(1,1).$\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=2x.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186175983 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488190 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186175983 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488222 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186176146 1050 660.0 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186177006 1064 "Suppose that we're given a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny' = F(x,y).\n$$\n\nBy factoring the function $F(x,y),$ we can often convert this differential equation to one that is of the form \n\n$$\ny' = f ( a x + b y ),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real constants. Then, using the substitution $z= ax+by,$ we can convert the equation to a separable differential equation. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'  = 9x^2 - 6xy + y^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is a perfect square. So, we factor it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=(3x-y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = 3x - y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 - \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \n\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 - \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our equation in terms of $z$ as\n\n$$\n3 - \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = z^2,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}   = 3-z^2.\n$$\n\nWe now have a separable differential equation." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186177006 1064 "Suppose that we're given a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny' = F(x,y).\n$$\n\nBy factoring the function $F(x,y),$ we can often convert this differential equation to one that is of the form \n\n$$\ny' = f ( a x + b y ),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real constants. Then, using the substitution $z= ax+by,$ we can convert the equation to a separable differential equation. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'  = 9x^2 - 6xy + y^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is a perfect square. So, we factor it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=(3x-y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = 3x - y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 - \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \n\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 - \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our equation in terms of $z$ as\n\n$$\n3 - \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = z^2,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}   = 3-z^2.\n$$\n\nWe now have a separable differential equation." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179465 1071 "Solve the equation $5ky-13=7$ for the variable $y$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179465 1071 "Solve the equation $5ky-13=7$ for the variable $y.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179465 1074 "We need to isolate $y$. To do this, we first add $13$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5ky - 13 &= 7 \\\\\n5ky - 13 + 13 &= 7 + 13 \\\\\n5ky &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $5k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5ky &= 20 \\\\\n\\frac{5ky}{5k} &= \\frac{20}{5k} \\\\\n\\frac{5ky}{5k} &= \\frac{20}{5k} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{20}{5k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{5k} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{5k} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4}{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179465 1074 "We need to isolate $y.$ To do this, we first add $13$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ky-13 & =7 \\\\ 5ky-13+13 & =7+13 \\\\ 5ky & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $5k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ky & =20 \\\\ \\frac{5ky}{5k} & =\\frac{20}{5k} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5k}y}{\\cancel{5k}} & =\\frac{20}{5k} \\\\ y & =\\frac{20}{5k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4}{5k} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 4}{\\cancel{5}k} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179466 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 34 kx + 9 = 2$ for the variable $x$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179466 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 34 kx + 9 = 2$ for the variable $x.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179466 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4}kx + 9 &= 2 \\\\\n4 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}kx + 9) &= 4 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n3kx + 36 &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to isolate $x$. To do this, we first subtract $36$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3kx + 36 &= 8 \\\\\n3kx + 36 - 36 &= 8 - 36 \\\\\n3kx &= -28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3kx &= -28 \\\\\n\\frac{3kx}{3k} &= \\frac{-28}{3k} \\\\\n\\frac{3kx}{3k} &= \\frac{-28}{3k} \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{28}{3k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179466 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}kx+9 & =2 \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}kx+9) & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3kx+36 & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to isolate $x.$ To do this, we first subtract $36$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3kx+36 & =8 \\\\ 3kx+36-36 & =8-36 \\\\ 3kx & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3kx & =-28 \\\\ \\frac{3kx}{3k} & =\\frac{-28}{3k} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3k}x}{\\cancel{3k}} & =\\frac{-28}{3k} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{28}{3k}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179467 1071 "Solve the equation $2 = wz - 2w +1$ for the variable $w$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179467 1071 "Solve the equation $2 = wz - 2w +1$ for the variable $w.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179467 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $w$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= wz - 2w + 1 \\\\\n2 - 1 &= wz - 2w + 1 - 1 \\\\\n1 &= wz - 2w\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $w$ in the expression on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= wz - 2w \\\\\n1 &= w(z - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(z-2)$ to isolate $w$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= w(z - 2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{z - 2} &= (w(z - 2))/(z - 2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{z - 2} &= (w(z - 2))/(z - 2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{z - 2} &= w \\\\\nw &= \\frac{1}{z - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that\n\n$$\nz-2\\neq 0\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179467 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $w$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =wz-2w+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =wz-2w+1-1 \\\\ 1 & =wz-2w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $w$ in the expression on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =wz-2w \\\\ 1 & =w(z-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(z-2)$ to isolate $w\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =w(z-2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =\\frac{w(z-2)}{z-2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =\\frac{w\\cancel{(z-2)}}{\\cancel{z-2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{z-2} & =w \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{z-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $z-2\\neq 0.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179468 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{3} = -\\dfrac{ax}{5}$ for the variable $x$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179468 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{3} = -\\dfrac{ax}{5}$ for the variable $x.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179468 1074 "We need to isolate $x$. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3} &= -\\frac{ax}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{-ax}{5} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 3 \\cdot (-ax) \\\\\n5 &= -3ax\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $-3a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= -3ax \\\\\n\\frac{5}{-3a} &= \\frac{-3ax}{-3a} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{-3a} &= \\frac{-3ax}{-3a} \\\\\n- \\frac{5}{3a} &= x \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{5}{3a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179468 1074 "We need to isolate $x.$ To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =-\\frac{ax}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{-ax}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 1 & =3\\cdot (-ax) \\\\ 5 & =-3ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $-3a\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-3ax \\\\ \\frac{5}{-3a} & =\\frac{-3ax}{-3a} \\\\ \\frac{5}{-3a} & =\\frac{\\cancel{-3a}x}{\\cancel{-3a}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3a} & =x \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{3a}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179469 1071 "Solve the equation $4ky=5y + 1$ for the variable $y$." 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179469 1071 "Solve the equation $4ky=5y + 1$ for the variable $y.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186179469 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $y$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $5y$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4ky &= 5y + 1 \\\\\n4ky - 5y &= 5y + 1 - 5y \\\\\n4ky - 5y &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $y$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $y$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $y$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4ky - 5y &= 1 \\\\\ny(4k - 5) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(4k -5)$ to isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(4k - 5) &= 1 \\\\\n(y(4k - 5))/((4k - 5)) &= (1)/((4k - 5)) \\\\\n(y(4k - 5))/((4k - 5)) &= \\frac{1}{4k - 5} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{4k - 5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that\n\n$$\n4k-5\\neq 0\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 false] [17592186179469 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $y$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $5y$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4ky & =5y+1 \\\\ 4ky-5y & =5y+1-5y \\\\ 4ky-5y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $y$ 