17 true] [17592186488174 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488174 1077 "selection" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488174 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487950 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488174 1079 #uuid "fdbf69e7-910a-5185-95d7-1b30300b0a57" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488175 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487789 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488175 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488175 1077 "field-1" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488175 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487789 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488175 1079 #uuid "fdd615eb-4925-5039-bbc3-b187bac64900" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487596 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487625 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487706 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487807 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488025 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1077 "selection" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487706 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488176 1079 #uuid "fdf5b574-2c56-5040-ad79-05467a068e0b" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488177 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487967 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488177 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488177 1077 "field-1" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488177 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487967 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488177 1079 #uuid "fe90978e-57e2-5d2c-a9e0-a9c95c24e6df" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488178 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487638 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488178 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488178 1077 "field-2" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488178 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487638 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488178 1079 #uuid "ffd90f0b-c8e3-5151-a11b-5c88bd560c16" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488083 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488179 1069 "q-215778" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488179 1071 "If $7pq+1=3q+8,$ then $q=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488179 1072 #uuid "03f30d76-6e4b-58e9-917f-05e941931839" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488179 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $q$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $1$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7pq+1 & =3q+8 \\\\ 7pq+1-1 & =3q+8-1 \\\\ 7pq & =3q+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3q$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7pq & =3q+7 \\\\ 7pq-3q & =3q+7-3q \\\\ 7pq-3q & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $q$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $q$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $q$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7pq-3q & =7 \\\\ (7p-3)q & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(7p-3)$ to isolate $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7p-3)q & =7 \\\\ \\frac{(7p-3)q}{7p-3} & =\\frac{7}{7p-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(7p-3)}q}{\\cancel{7p-3}} & =\\frac{7}{7p-3} \\\\ q & =\\frac{7}{7p-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $7p-3\\ne 0.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488162 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488180 1069 "q-133663" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488180 1071 "If $\\frac{4}{3}=5vs,$ then $s=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488180 1072 #uuid "06c556aa-e44d-528e-81fb-0f187fd8faf9" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488180 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & =5vs \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} & =3\\cdot 5vs \\\\ 4 & =15vs\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to isolate $s.$ To do this, we divide both sides of the equation by $15v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =15vs \\\\ \\frac{4}{15v} & =\\frac{15vs}{15v} \\\\ \\frac{4}{15v} & =\\frac{\\cancel{15v}s}{\\cancel{15v}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{15v} & =s \\\\ s & =\\frac{4}{15v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488100 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488181 1069 "q-224989" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488181 1071 "Consider the Poisson distribution $\\text{Po}(12).$ Rounding to $4$ decimal digits, fill in the missing value in the corresponding table for the cumulative distribution function below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0203\\,$ | $\\,0.0458\\,$ | $\\,0.0895\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $\\,0.2424\\,$ | $\\,0.3472\\,$ | $\\,0.4616\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488181 1072 #uuid "06c6c230-3fa4-5b71-a2c3-1d7beda8e3c7" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488181 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function for the Poisson distribution $\\text{Po}(\\lambda )$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,3,…\\,.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $\\lambda =12.$ \n\nTherefore, we compute the missing element of the table as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(8) & =P(X\\le 8) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =F(7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =0.0895+\\frac{{12}^{8}\\cdot {e}^{-12}}{8!} \\\\  & ≈0.1550\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488059 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488113 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488124 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488182 1069 "q-223601" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488182 1071 "![](images/58/5835b3fdf2fccfd3b27f326bd90188a8382ea826a019c161e65c8a58376b3d7f.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488182 1072 #uuid "0794aaff-8257-5ff5-95bd-c8b72d62b6ae" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488182 1074 "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(-4,-2).$\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(4,4).$ However note that this point is not actually included in the graph.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $4,$ including $-2$ but not including $4.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y<4,$ which can also be written as $-2\\le f(x)<4.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488130 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488183 1069 "q-171876" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488183 1071 "Consider a triangle $△RST,$ where $m∠R=2x+{3}^{∘},$ $m∠S=4x$ and $m∠T=x+{2}^{∘}.$ Find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488183 1072 #uuid "08ae86f4-7baa-5fd1-bf6d-dfc10d8069d6" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488183 1074 "The sum of the measures of the interior angles must be ${180}^{∘}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+{3}^{∘})+(4x)+(x+{2}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x+{5}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={175}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={25}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488108 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488184 1069 "q-214556" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488184 1071 "If $2=-5ab,$ then $a=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488184 1072 #uuid "0b499b93-e0d8-519c-9fd8-51ac13674a1f" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488184 1074 "We need to isolate $a.$ To do this, we divide both sides of the equation by $-5b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-5ab \\\\ \\frac{2}{-5b} & =\\frac{-5ab}{-5b} \\\\ \\frac{2}{-5b} & =\\frac{\\cancel{-5b}a}{\\cancel{-5b}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5b} & =a \\\\ a & =-\\frac{2}{5b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488090 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488114 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488169 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488185 1069 "q-236721" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488185 1071 "![](images/df/dff5e6055378143e81d4771d8beeb21e2b9b287b6fb630779885a1bce288d77d.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488185 1072 #uuid "117f6ecc-4203-5323-8f20-261b6a681a27" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488185 1074 "We find the ranges of the individual pieces and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ is $(-3,-1)$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-1,3)$ is $[-2,2)$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $(-3,-1)\\cup [-2,2)=(-3,2),$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-3<f(x)<2.\n$$\n\n**Note:** From the graph, we see that the values $y=-3$ and $y=2$ are NOT valid outputs of the function $y=f(x).$ Therefore, we use \"less than\" $(<)$ instead of \"less than or equal to\" $(\\le )$ at these endpoints." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488173 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488186 1069 "q-140129" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488186 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488186 1072 #uuid "11fac3f6-18af-5205-bfbf-77c7cc7c000b" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488186 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488070 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488187 1069 "q-78011" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488187 1071 "Margaret is studying and analyzing a culture of bacteria. From a certain moment, she notices that the number of bacteria $B(t)$ in the culture grows according to the formula $B(t)=500\\cdot {2}^{t},$ where $t$ denotes the time in hours. How many bacteria will there be after $4$ hours?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488187 1072 #uuid "169115e9-c4da-5b2d-98db-fdbf90bfcfbf" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488187 1074 "To get the number of bacteria in the culture after $4$ hours, we evaluate the function at $t=4.$ Substituting $t=4$ into the function $B(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =500\\cdot (2)^{(4)} \\\\  & =500\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =8\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $8\\,000$ bacteria in the culture after $4$ hours." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488127 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488188 1069 "q-132647" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488188 1071 "${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488188 1072 #uuid "1781d10d-4447-567d-8dfd-9f16c098a159" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488188 1074 "![](images/d2/d2ef7f656cf706636c82e1ef41ae338a596fff5142962a74875f762301937932.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le y\\le -1,\\,1\\le x\\le -\\frac{4}{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{y}\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{4}{x},\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{4}{x}\\le y\\le -1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/33/3338ee0f8bdc6f18bb5a56b12a36d31d79c87db6c39686adc7ae7c00efb9d526.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}[{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-4/x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{x}-1)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+2}-2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1+2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2}-2 \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488104 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488189 1069 "q-38799" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488189 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488189 1072 #uuid "18010b03-c75c-5a16-8ddc-495cf577e016" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488189 1074 "![](images/54/54727a51e88b3543726b61606a0f41b9a8cf5ec46ef4c69d7233fd54a4b50242.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/95/956aaf81973d9a2b3e56082ecae8e654eee55d6f99c42dd433d126632d7c7262.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{y}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{{y}^{4}}}{3}[{x}^{3}]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,{e}^{{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[{e}^{{y}^{4}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488136 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488190 1069 "q-149760" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488190 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2+4{x}^{2}-4xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488190 1072 #uuid "19e48035-430d-58ef-92e6-a92cd0b0c10e" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488190 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2+(2x-y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2+{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}={z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{z} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2x-y}.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488121 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488191 1069 "q-77587" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488191 1071 "![](images/b0/b05ae89c373b6ce1ed9326099dd2f86f9d48220b36d29bf36d37a0c71d2ebaa9.png)\n\nClassify the triangle above." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488191 1072 #uuid "1c3543cf-d292-519c-b94c-943e42d7f653" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488191 1074 "In the above triangle, we see two congruent sides,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{AB}≅\\overset{―}{AC},\n$$\n\nbut the third side is not congruent:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{AB}≇\\overset{―}{BC}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since only two sides are congruent, $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{BC}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488063 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488192 1069 "q-135478" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488192 1071 "If $u+\\frac{v}{2}=1,$ then $u=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488192 1072 #uuid "1f182666-9412-501e-b641-7f7f9b2222ad" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488192 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $u.$ To do this, we treat $v$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nIn this case, we simply subtract $\\frac{v}{2}$ from both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+\\frac{v}{2} & =1 \\\\ u+\\frac{v}{2}-\\frac{v}{2} & =1-\\frac{v}{2} \\\\ u+0 & =1-\\frac{v}{2} \\\\ u & =1-\\frac{v}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488152 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488193 1069 "q-61716" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488193 1071 "If $h(t)=128\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{t},$ then $h(3)=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488193 1072 #uuid "20bd28d6-3021-5539-a5f9-9ec2d307a221" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488193 1074 "To calculate $h(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $h(t).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =128\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\  & =128\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}) \\\\  & =128\\cdot \\frac{1}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{64} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488109 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488194 1069 "q-135010" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488194 1071 "If $\\frac{x}{2}-5y=2y$ then $x=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488194 1072 #uuid "2257e81b-6df1-53bd-bf2f-94399c3d9e90" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488194 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $x.$ To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we add $5y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-5y & =2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}-5y+5y & =2y+5y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =2y+5y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =7y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =7y \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot 7y \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot 7y \\\\ x & =2\\cdot 7y \\\\ x & =14y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488146 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488195 1069 "q-123880" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488195 1071 "Which of the following regions is connected?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488195 1072 #uuid "244776d4-c112-511f-a089-aa2567ad8e69" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488195 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/4d/4d7d916e62541ad8d41f6043c0d0e2f511f0b79d036f6fce6b4ce4500a357023.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488138 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488196 1069 "q-104488" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488196 1071 "Solve for $w$ where $\\frac{w-3}{7}=\\frac{1-2w}{6}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488196 1072 #uuid "246474dc-65ca-5dbb-80ff-e29f8ffb3cb5" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488196 1074 "First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w-3}{7} & =\\frac{1-2w}{6} \\\\ (w-3)\\cdot 6 & =7\\cdot (1-2w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(w-3)\\cdot 6 & =7\\cdot (1-2w) \\\\ 6w-18 & =7-14w \\\\ 6w-18+14w & =7-14w+14w \\\\ 20w-18 & =7 \\\\ 20w-18+18 & =7+18 \\\\ 20w & =25 \\\\ \\frac{20w}{20} & =\\frac{25}{20} \\\\ w & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488087 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488197 1069 "q-235771" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488197 1071 "![](images/4f/4f01fa959d7370cecc91a7c54575b8d1022cbbb73d422f381e1766de03801822.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $z=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488197 1072 #uuid "25ca3a8d-acc3-599f-b660-f34015483f42" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488197 1074 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{40}^{∘}+z+3z & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {40}^{∘}+4z & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4z & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={35}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488170 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488198 1069 "q-253865" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488198 1071 "![](images/5e/5e39b8db15f47b0c07efec1b364f6fda7b3b7991ba13d71125505b1172937888.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488198 1072 #uuid "28cb24da-7ead-51cb-82fe-c700e5c2c25f" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488198 1074 "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no lower bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is smaller than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=-\\infty .$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488103 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488199 1069 "q-82253" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488199 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488199 1072 #uuid "291bba76-b18a-519c-8c81-930cc19f299e" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488199 1074 "![](images/83/8359a189981b238d0851324a896ed0a3a9aa0620e8cf4d38228752ff484b4cd0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2(x-2)^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{4-y}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\\le x\\le \\frac{4-y}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/be/bee5de8ca9011672a159686d1547a909e5288097b15241f5004e5c788459ee70.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2(x-2)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y/2}}^{(4-y)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488168 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488200 1069 "q-98397" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488200 1071 "Which of the following regions is connected?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488200 1072 #uuid "29b45a36-fdc6-5e26-9a19-0e7651460f03" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488200 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/44/4496057807b8ae67cf1c8687a1118e0cbcb797726c7ecff1029f34397f521cfb.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488096 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488201 1069 "q-98391" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488201 1071 "Which of the following regions is connected?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488201 1072 #uuid "2d2171f1-1144-5e47-b93c-119cd4a73009" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488201 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/08/0841ee8ea9f2d7454ee852041b3dfb470b6cf17237cec54960f400da337910c0.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488077 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488202 1069 "q-98680" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488202 1071 "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488202 1072 #uuid "353c7243-8109-54a9-8124-e25ba2a61d98" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488202 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:  \n\n![](images/37/37597a2cecae00cca3eacec7265913070de9343be6afb9108644d6c8d0c1d93b.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488084 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488203 1069 "q-36989" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488203 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -10 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces ${V}_{-3}$ and ${V}_{-1}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$, respectively?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488203 1072 #uuid "3be1ddbd-2595-5ba8-8ba6-f7f8cb00a8ea" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488203 1074 "First, we write down the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -8 & -10 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -8 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+8)=0 \\\\ -(\\lambda +3)({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3)=0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1)=0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}(\\lambda +1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ (double root) and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1$ (simple root).\n\nLet's now consider the eigenspaces corresponding to each eigenvector:\n\n- First, we consider $\\lambda =-1.$ Since ${V}_{-1}$ corresponds to a simple root, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-1})=1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we consider $\\lambda =-3,$ which is a repeated root of the characteristic equation. To find the dimension of ${V}_{-3},$ we row-reduce the matrix $(A-(-3)I)\\text{x}=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -10 & -5\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -10 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-3})=2.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488073 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488204 1069 "q-214615" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488204 1071 "If $-\\frac{3xy}{10}=\\frac{1}{2},$ then $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488204 1072 #uuid "3c956651-7831-59be-948a-98c8e15c7d28" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488204 1074 "We need to isolate $y.$ To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3xy}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{-3xy}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3xy) & =10\\cdot 1 \\\\ -6xy & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $-6x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6xy & =10 \\\\ \\frac{-6xy}{-6x} & =\\frac{10}{-6x} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-6x}\\,y}{\\cancel{-6x}} & =\\frac{10}{-6x} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{10}{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 3x} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3x} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488066 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488205 1069 "q-225064" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488205 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(5.5)$ given below, compute $P(1<X<7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0266\\,$ | $\\,0.0884\\,$ | $\\,0.2017\\,$ | $\\,0.3575\\,$ | $\\,0.5289\\,$ | $\\,0.6860\\,$ | $\\,0.8095\\,$ |\n\n$P(1<X<7)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488205 1072 #uuid "3fd62c03-4c13-5eaa-a871-a2d0ae21b2c3" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488205 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(6) & =P(X<7) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\})+P(X\\in \\{2,3,4,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 1)+P(1<X<7) \\\\  & =F(1)+P(1<X<7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<7) & =F(6)-F(1) \\\\  & =0.6860-0.0266 \\\\  & =0.6594.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488110 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488206 1069 "q-224999" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488206 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(5)$ given below, compute $P(X<7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.2650\\,$ | $\\,0.4405\\,$ | $\\,0.6160\\,$ | $\\,0.7622\\,$ | $\\,0.8666\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ | $\\,0.9682\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<7)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488206 1072 #uuid "47d084d0-558c-55b5-8190-75e291aec240" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488206 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<7) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & =F(6) \\\\  & =0.7622.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488106 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488207 1069 "q-67649" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488207 1071 "![](images/17/171cff06ab08f9ce49acd6b203c074ec329abdc35caca357221329980c85f531.png)\n\nOrder the angles of the triangle $△ABC$ from smallest to largest." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488207 1072 #uuid "4b8c6582-8730-58e4-8a1f-1712623b6eac" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488207 1074 "The shortest side is $\\overset{―}{AC},$ so the smallest interior angle is $∠B.$\n\nThe longest side is $\\overset{―}{BC},$ which means that the opposite angle, $∠A$, must be the largest one.\n\nTherefore, the required order must be\n\n$$\n∠B,\\,∠C,\\,∠A.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488088 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488102 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488126 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488135 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488166 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488167 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1069 "q-343939" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1071 "Consider the set $B=(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{2}\\,].$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1072 #uuid "506b27d0-9ecb-54b1-8cb0-ba21f98fbff3" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488208 1074 "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded below, $B$ has no lower bounds, so $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{2}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(B)=min\\,[\\sqrt{2},\\infty )=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488074 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488209 1069 "q-225079" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488209 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(3)$ given below, compute $P(X>5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0498\\,$ | $\\,0.1991\\,$ | $\\,0.4232\\,$ | $\\,0.6472\\,$ | $\\,0.8153\\,$ | $\\,0.9161\\,$ | $\\,0.9665\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488209 1072 #uuid "51b5f656-5014-5979-a4cf-5d15629f454f" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488209 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =1-F(5) \\\\  & =1-0.9161 \\\\  & =0.0839.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488150 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488210 1069 "q-253900" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488210 1071 "![](images/40/40c6f3bbfcbf08db07501076a43ba011da20c57c70865ec2700334d560936eae.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488210 1072 #uuid "56f49ac3-e6b5-56aa-af36-053b89a652ef" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488210 1074 "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $y$ for any $y>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488128 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488211 1069 "q-77588" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488211 1071 "![](images/d8/d828b48f347d79f8a6536ca178f5bb24bd46f6d35c278ca21bebdbb9a1b86750.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements best describes the triangle above?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488211 1072 #uuid "589a66a6-0e3d-5725-8cc4-53657e5b6c5b" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488211 1074 "In the above triangle, we see two congruent sides,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{AB}≅\\overset{―}{BC},\n$$\n\nbut the third side is not congruent:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC}≇\\overset{―}{AC}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since only two sides are congruent, $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AC}.$\n\nOn the other hand, $∠B$ is a right angle. Therefore, $△ABC$ is a right isosceles triangle." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488081 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488212 1069 "q-67648" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488212 1071 "![](images/6b/6be28d1022cecbbd72a6dd6156f2bc2b6f653416607ae8fa409c25b3e97fdf35.png)\n\nOrder the sides of the triangle $△ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488212 1072 #uuid "5ebcfbce-bc83-5ed3-8c76-d526d36ab2c8" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488212 1074 "First, let's find the missing angle $B.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {40}^{∘}+m∠B+{95}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{135}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠C$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{AB}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $∠A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC<AC<AB\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC},\\,\\overset{―}{AC},\\,\\overset{―}{AB}.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488161 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488213 1069 "q-133899" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488213 1071 "If $\\frac{2km}{3}=\\frac{k+m}{5},$ then $k=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488213 1072 #uuid "5eedbb0b-2f52-5239-8343-cadc5e74eba1" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488213 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $k$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2km}{3} & =\\frac{k+m}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2km & =3\\cdot (k+m) \\\\ 10km & =3k+3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3k$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10km & =3k+3m \\\\ 10km-3k & =3k+3m-3k \\\\ 10km-3k & =3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $k$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $k$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $k$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10km-3k & =3m \\\\ k(10m-3) & =3m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(10m-3)$ to isolate $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(10m-3) & =3m \\\\ \\frac{k\\cancel{(10m-3)}}{\\cancel{10m-3}} & =\\frac{3m}{10m-3} \\\\ k & =\\frac{3m}{10m-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $10m-3\\ne 0.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488057 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488158 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488178 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488214 1069 "q-236066" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488214 1071 "![](images/96/9676726ee0d5b979eb326d262856e37618f09e505093d4997d168826a173c05b.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\le f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488214 1072 #uuid "5fec27c7-ab59-5059-80aa-0971460daa74" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488214 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-6.$ This occurs when $x=-2.$\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $2.$ This occurs when $x=2.$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-6$ and $2,$ including $-6$ and $2.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-6\\le y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as $-6\\le f(x)\\le 2.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488176 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488215 1069 "q-69816" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488215 1071 "Solve the equation ${4}^{x}-{4}^{5}=0.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488215 1072 #uuid "61bdcaea-72dd-5dae-b8e1-c1f54914fb34" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488215 1074 "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding ${4}^{5}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x}-{4}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {4}^{x}-\\cancel{{4}^{5}}+\\cancel{{4}^{5}} & =0+{4}^{5} \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & ={4}^{5} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488148 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488216 1069 "q-77542" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488216 1071 "The measure of one angle in a triangle is four times the measure of another angle, and the measure of the third angle is ${85}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the smallest angle?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488216 1072 #uuid "627791d7-dc7c-50b5-b4d8-fdfd901f4303" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488216 1074 "Let $x$ be the measure of the first angle. Then the measure of the second angle is $4x.$\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles must be ${180}^{∘}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4x+{85}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x+{85}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={95}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={19}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, the measure of the smallest angle is ${19}^{∘}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488151 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488217 1069 "q-142891" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488217 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{9}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(2\\,|\\,3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488217 1072 #uuid "654ece2b-abda-569c-8738-da98c426c46e" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488217 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(2\\,|\\,3)=P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=2)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,2)+f(3,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}+\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(3,2)=\\frac{9}{20}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $f(3,2)=\\frac{9}{20}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(2\\,|\\,3) & =P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{20})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488093 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488218 1069 "q-111696" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488218 1071 "Solve the equation ${5}^{13}={5}^{x}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488218 1072 #uuid "6757a4c2-eb31-57ff-8c26-afeb2acc7f66" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488218 1074 "Since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{13} & ={5}^{x} \\\\ 13 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=13.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488056 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488080 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488123 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488219 1069 "q-236385" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488219 1071 "![](images/a4/a4b653263aa47cfeecceeda20adfe5851b81d0a7c4fc6457ef87f45ea65ca27d.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488219 1072 #uuid "68f16f52-3b3f-54c0-8129-b8dd5925efc4" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488219 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs when $x\\to \\infty .$ However, note  that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $0$ and $\\infty ,$ but not including $0.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $0<y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as \n$0<f(x)<\\infty .$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488061 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488220 1069 "q-98687" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488220 1071 "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488220 1072 #uuid "6a1d02fe-8171-5afa-b624-bbcbed75b5b0" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488220 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following: \n\n![](images/65/65e1c034f836802bb8127e754f0e1cb303144468b041d51da2ee1b8a89de1132.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488082 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488221 1069 "q-133790" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488221 1071 "If $10=3rs+2s-2,$ then $s=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488221 1072 #uuid "6b9f4cd9-9a85-5f2d-9eac-f617eac7ba39" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488221 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $s$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we add $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =3rs+2s-2 \\\\ 10+2 & =3rs+2s-2+2 \\\\ 12 & =3rs+2s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $s$ in the expression on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =3rs+2s \\\\ 12 & =s(3r+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(3r+2)$ to isolate $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =s(3r+2) \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =\\frac{s(3r+2)}{3r+2} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =\\frac{s\\cancel{(3r+2)}}{\\cancel{3r+2}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{3r+2} & =s \\\\ s & =\\frac{12}{3r+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3r+2\\ne 0.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488172 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488222 1069 "q-108772" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488222 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}+8xy+4{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(g(x,y))=2x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488222 1072 #uuid "6beef5a1-8cca-5685-897f-0ef5f4dc810f" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488222 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4(x+y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =4{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=4{z}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{4{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2z) & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2z) & =2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+2y)=2x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=2x+2y.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488055 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488223 1069 "q-225004" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488223 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(10)$ given below, compute $P(X=7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0103\\,$ | $\\,0.0293\\,$ | $\\,0.0671\\,$ | $\\,0.1301\\,$ | $\\,0.2202\\,$ | $\\,0.3328\\,$ | $\\,0.4579\\,$ |\n\n$P(X=7)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488223 1072 #uuid "6ed46a6a-33c2-5d2f-895f-2046417a88d9" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488223 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7) & =P(X\\le 7) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,7\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,6\\})+P(X=7) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 6)+P(X=7) \\\\  & =F(6)+P(X=7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=7) & =F(7)-F(6) \\\\  & =0.2202-0.1301 \\\\  & =0.0901.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488068 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488224 1069 "q-118189" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488224 1071 "![](images/e7/e7eb91b29c5a284d1ec7d487df5693dd5b10a18ff21dc1ef6223e47d32474e5b.png)\n\nOrder the angles of the triangle $△XYZ$ from smallest to largest." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488224 1072 #uuid "72d7422a-3ddb-5828-b3d9-7f65634fb116" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488224 1074 "The longest side is $\\overset{―}{XZ},$ which means that the opposite angle, $∠Y$, must be the largest one.\n\nThe shortest side is $\\overset{―}{YZ},$ so the smallest interior angle is $∠X.$\n\nTherefore, the required order must be\n\n$$\n∠X,\\,∠Z,\\,∠Y\\,.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488134 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488225 1069 "q-124773" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488225 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+2x\\ge 1\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-2x\\ne 1\\}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488225 1072 #uuid "7361f82f-c91c-52b9-80ec-852a74fe03bc" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488225 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge -2x+1.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes,\n\n- Region II is simply connected.  It's a plane region (a circle) without holes.\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=2x+1.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488141 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488226 1069 "q-253548" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488226 1071 "Consider the set $D,$ defined as\n\n$$\nD=\\{n-{n}^{2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $D?$\n\n$inf(D)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488226 1072 #uuid "73aef377-5ed1-5df9-8acf-ea492a3b5fe7" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488226 1074 "The infimum of a set $D$ is the greatest lower bound of $D.$\n\nIf $D$ is a finite set, the infimum of $D$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $D:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2-{2}^{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3-{3}^{2} & =-6 \\\\ 4-{4}^{2} & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(D) & =min\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488153 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488227 1069 "q-253141" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488227 1071 "![](images/81/8152677cc952ca219aa9395f3d1a9acbc9379b1cffd0b0f0e6bd0f3f0148632a.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488227 1072 #uuid "75529c50-5084-5200-a1d5-34d0fbf018a7" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488227 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488098 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488228 1069 "q-164306" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488228 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}-\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488228 1072 #uuid "756c31bd-d0b5-518e-8fa8-bf3e64b797d5" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488228 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}-\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16} & =-1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1 & =\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16} & =(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1 \\\\ (z+5)^{2} & =16((x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1) \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 4\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488137 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488229 1069 "q-133665" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488229 1071 "If $2=\\frac{3}{10}hk,$ then $k=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488229 1072 #uuid "76378346-6bd7-5f90-bb11-bc03bf523eef" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488229 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{3}{10}hk \\\\ 10\\cdot 2 & =10\\cdot \\frac{3}{10}hk \\\\ 20 & =3hk\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to isolate $k.$ To do this, we divide both sides of the equation by $3h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =3hk \\\\ \\frac{20}{3h} & =\\frac{3hk}{3h} \\\\ \\frac{20}{3h} & =\\frac{\\cancel{3h}k}{\\cancel{3h}} \\\\ \\frac{20}{3h} & =k \\\\ k & =\\frac{20}{3h}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488116 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488230 1069 "q-224998" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488230 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(12)$ given below, compute $P(X<11).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $13$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0895\\,$ | $\\,0.1550\\,$ | $\\,0.2424\\,$ | $\\,0.3472\\,$ | $\\,0.4616\\,$ | $\\,0.5760\\,$ | $\\,0.6815\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<11)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488230 1072 #uuid "77a09fd4-a75e-503e-85e7-5369cc0e38f9" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488230 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<11) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,10\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 10) \\\\  & =F(10) \\\\  & =0.3472.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488171 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488231 1069 "q-215810" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488231 1071 "If $\\frac{7mn+2n}{4}=\\frac{3m}{7},$ then $m=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488231 1072 #uuid "77fe19c1-2d2e-5ba1-9d49-d82b50f369df" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488231 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $m$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7mn+2n}{4} & =\\frac{3m}{7} \\\\ 7\\cdot (7mn+2n) & =4\\cdot 3m \\\\ 49mn+14n & =12m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $49mn$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49mn+14n & =12m \\\\ 49mn+14n-49mn & =12m-49mn \\\\ 14n & =12m-49mn\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $m$ are on the right-hand side, and all terms independent of $m$ are on the left-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $m$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14n & =12m-49mn \\\\ 14n & =(12-49n)m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(12-49n)$ to isolate $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14n & =(12-49n)m \\\\ \\frac{14n}{12-49n} & =\\frac{\\cancel{(12-49n)}m}{\\cancel{12-49n}} \\\\ \\frac{14n}{12-49n} & =m \\\\ m & =\\frac{14n}{12-49n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $12-49n\\ne 0.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488094 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488232 1069 "q-27488" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488232 1071 "Let $△ABC$ be a triangle such that $m∠A=2m∠C$ and  $m∠B=m∠A+m∠C.$ \nWhich of the following statements is true?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488232 1072 #uuid "78cb72f5-fa99-5a05-bb80-ea35eabe4485" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488232 1074 "Let $x$ be the measure of the angle $∠C,$ then $m∠A=2x$ and $m∠B=2x+x=3x.$\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles is ${180}^{∘}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+3x+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =2\\cdot {30}^{∘}={60}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠B & =3\\cdot {30}^{∘}={90}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠C & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $△ABC$ is a right triangle because it has a right angle ($m∠B={90}^{∘}$)." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488079 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488233 1069 "q-27874" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488233 1071 "Solve for $j$ where $\\frac{j-4}{5}=\\frac{8+j}{4}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488233 1072 #uuid "80d602e5-e159-5437-af51-932d09f28858" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488233 1074 "First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{j-4}{5} & =\\frac{8+j}{4} \\\\ (j-4)\\cdot 4 & =5\\cdot (8+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(j-4)\\cdot 4 & =5\\cdot (8+j) \\\\ 4j-16 & =40+5j \\\\ 4j-16-4j & =40+5j-4j \\\\ -16 & =40+j \\\\ -16-40 & =40+j-40 \\\\ -56 & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $j=-56.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488097 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488234 1069 "q-75488" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488234 1071 "If $\\frac{12nx}{2}=\\frac{4}{7},$ then $x=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488234 1072 #uuid "821390a9-15de-5be4-b110-42ad4543a1a6" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488234 1074 "We need to isolate $x.$ To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12nx}{2} & =\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ 7\\cdot 12nx & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ 84nx & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $84n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84nx & =8 \\\\ \\frac{84nx}{84n} & =\\frac{8}{84n} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{84n}x}{\\cancel{84n}} & =\\frac{8}{84n} \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{84n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2}{4\\cdot 21n} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\cancel{4}\\cdot 2}{\\cancel{4}\\cdot 21n} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{21n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488111 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488235 1069 "q-116736" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488235 1071 "![](images/ad/adcb81c2fd7553c458ce24b8668f383f9b54024225a0e88b548140cb19d3e28a.png)\n\nCalculate the value of $x.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488235 1072 #uuid "8267e27a-d7e7-55df-b9b5-74944df1c40e" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488235 1074 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{60}^{∘}+x+(x+{10}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {70}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={110}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488091 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488236 1069 "q-224482" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488236 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(X=5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0424\\,$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ | $\\,0.7166\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488236 1072 #uuid "88a38ed6-7fa9-5b5b-87c9-ffc4db1074d1" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488236 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,4\\})+P(X=5) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =F(4)+P(X=5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =F(5)-F(4) \\\\  & =0.1912-0.0996 \\\\  & =0.0916.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488177 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488237 1069 "q-253546" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488237 1071 "What is the supremum of the set $C=\\{-4,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,1\\}?$\n\n$sup(C)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488237 1072 #uuid "8b620bc4-f772-542c-8902-9f29e69330eb" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488237 1074 "The supremum of a set $C$ is the smallest upper bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the supremum of $C$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(C) & =max\\{-4,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,1\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488069 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488238 1069 "q-214561" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488238 1071 "If $5=-14ab-2,$ then $a=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488238 1072 #uuid "8bf4f7f2-b45d-5aef-8d47-16e8d1e1f900" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488238 1074 "We need to isolate $a.$ To do this, we first add $2$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-14ab-2 \\\\ 5+2 & =-14ab-2+2 \\\\ 7 & =-14ab\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $-14b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =-14ab \\\\ \\frac{7}{-14b} & =\\frac{-14ab}{-14b} \\\\ \\frac{7}{-14b} & =\\frac{\\cancel{-14b}a}{\\cancel{-14b}} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{14b} & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{7\\cdot 2b} & =a \\\\ -\\frac{\\cancel{7}}{\\cancel{7}\\cdot 2b} & =a \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2b} & =a \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488160 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488239 1069 "q-2611" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488239 1071 "If $6x+3y=3$ then $y=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488239 1072 #uuid "8cb21341-1ec0-588f-b6f2-da7ac9131ac4" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488239 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $y.$ To do this, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $6x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+3y & =3 \\\\ 6x+3y-6x & =3-6x \\\\ 6x-6x+3y & =3-6x \\\\ 3y & =3-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y & =3-6x \\\\ 3y & =3(1-2x) \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & =\\frac{3(1-2x)}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}(1-2x)}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ y & =1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488132 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488240 1069 "q-79" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488240 1071 "Solve the equation $3\\cdot {5}^{s}=75.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488240 1072 #uuid "936aa682-294b-52d5-96a5-a38d57d9bb85" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488240 1074 "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by dividing both sides by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{s} & =75 \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot {5}^{s}}{3} & =\\frac{75}{3} \\\\ {5}^{s} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $25$ as ${5}^{2}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{s} & =25 \\\\ {5}^{s} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{s} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ s & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $s=2.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488119 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488241 1069 "q-223125" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488241 1071 "If ${10}^{x}={10}^{5},$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488241 1072 #uuid "9744b183-f539-5606-a140-f9ad45bfd0fe" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488241 1074 "Since both sides of the equation share the same base $(10)$, we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x} & ={10}^{5} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488064 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488242 1069 "q-225081" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488242 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(X>6).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0030\\,$ | $\\,0.0138\\,$ | $\\,0.0424\\,$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ |\n\n$P(X>6)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488242 1072 #uuid "9ba81e5b-3639-5408-81fe-d49741329753" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488242 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & =1-F(6) \\\\  & =1-0.3134 \\\\  & =0.6866.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488142 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488243 1069 "q-176623" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488243 1071 "Given that $\\frac{2x+1}{3}=\\frac{y-x}{2},$ express $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488243 1072 #uuid "a062c0fa-ba5c-540d-bad1-e5dc2b1fdc72" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488243 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $x.$ First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{3} & =\\frac{y-x}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (2x+1) & =3\\cdot (y-x) \\\\ 4x+2 & =3y-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $2$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & =3y-3x \\\\ 4x+2-2 & =3y-3x-2 \\\\ 4x & =3y-3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $3x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =3y-3x-2 \\\\ 4x+3x & =3y-3x-2+3x \\\\ 7x & =3y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & =3y-2 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =\\frac{3y-2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & =\\frac{3y-2}{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3y-2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488145 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488244 1069 "q-178606" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488244 1071 "Given that $4x-y=7-5y,$ express $y$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488244 1072 #uuid "a390d02a-3ecf-5fc0-8eb1-0f04dc4eff6f" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488244 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $y.$ To do this, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we add $5y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-y & =7-5y \\\\ 4x-y+5y & =7-5y+5y \\\\ 4x+4y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & =7 \\\\ 4x+4y-4x & =7-4x \\\\ 4y & =7-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & =7-4x \\\\ \\frac{4y}{4} & =\\frac{7-4x}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}y}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\frac{7-4x}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{7-4x}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488052 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488245 1069 "q-98392" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488245 1071 "Which of the following regions is connected?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488245 1072 #uuid "a4b8bc46-7868-52e5-a7b3-40602e3b7509" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488245 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/dd/dd767429c321d02dc6edf559127ff8ecc733bd5ecdf243b79f7b671fd78085c4.png)" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488115 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488246 1069 "q-140839" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488246 1071 "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488246 1072 #uuid "a57f1e60-3a1f-5809-920d-a2e3527a6835" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488246 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488072 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488247 1069 "q-64042" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488247 1071 "Given that $g(t)=5\\cdot {4}^{t},$ find $t$ such that $g(t)=80.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488247 1072 #uuid "a6431deb-d4ee-50b8-b78e-d09da4574bf6" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488247 1074 "First, we substitute $g(t)=80$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot {4}^{t}=80\n$$\n\nWe can divide both sides of the equation by the constant factor of $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\cdot {4}^{t}}{5} & =\\frac{80}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot {4}^{t}}{\\cancel{5}} & =16 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $t$ using the fact that $16$ is a power of $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{t} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488075 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488248 1069 "q-77535" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488248 1071 "![](images/00/0086a1608c554544cdfc3337e5df136ada8986743ebc2329f262ffc90dbe28e7.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $A$ given that $m∠B=m∠C.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488248 1072 #uuid "a74d4f60-688d-582a-8efa-ef124e45e804" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488248 1074 "Using $m∠B=m∠C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & =m∠C \\\\ z+{10}^{∘} & =2z \\\\ z & ={10}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, substituting $z={10}^{∘}$ in the expression for $m∠C,$ we get\n\n$$\nm∠C=2({10}^{∘})={20}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find $m∠A$ using the fact that the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+2m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+2\\cdot {20}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488139 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488249 1069 "q-133775" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488249 1071 "If $5tx-3x=2,$ then $x=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488249 1072 #uuid "a7db436d-ecf4-5342-922a-617ed93580d3" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488249 1074 "First, we factor out $x$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5tx-3x & =2 \\\\ x(5t-3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(5t-3)$ to isolate $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(5t-3) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x(5t-3)}{5t-3} & =\\frac{2}{5t-3} \\\\ \\frac{x\\cancel{(5t-3)}}{\\cancel{5t-3}} & =\\frac{2}{5t-3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5t-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $5t-3\\ne 0.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488147 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488250 1069 "q-225057" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488250 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(6.5)$ given below, compute $P(3\\le X\\le 7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0430\\,$ | $\\,0.1118\\,$ | $\\,0.2237\\,$ | $\\,0.3690\\,$ | $\\,0.5265\\,$ | $\\,0.6728\\,$ | $\\,0.7916\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488250 1072 #uuid "a9431e7c-b663-534c-b1f2-e2cde30992c6" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488250 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7) & =P(X\\le 7) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,7\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\})+P(X\\in \\{3,4,5,6,7\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2)+P(3\\le X\\le 7) \\\\  & =F(2)+P(3\\le X\\le 7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 7) & =F(7)-F(2) \\\\  & =0.6728-0.0430 \\\\  & =0.6298.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488105 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488251 1069 "q-45715" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488251 1071 "The population $Z(t)$ of zebras in a certain nature reserve is given by $Z(t)=10\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time in years since the reserve was opened. How many zebras will be in the reserve after $4$ years?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488251 1072 #uuid "b0a1af6d-481b-5cb9-88d9-fa115158484b" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488251 1074 "To get the number of zebras after $4$ years, we evaluate the function at $t=4.$ Substituting $t=4$ into the function $Z(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =10\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{(4)} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\cdot 2.073\\,6 \\\\  & =20\\,736.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, there will be $20\\,736$ zebras in the nature reserve." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488095 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488252 1069 "q-235974" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488252 1071 "![](images/d2/d24c4077069b1de16c64e3179b8e60094e0440ed0a95ac3525297af85899bec2.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $C$ given that $m∠A=m∠B.$\n\n$m∠C=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488252 1072 #uuid "b302caff-8016-5932-bece-17fa5667969f" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488252 1074 "Using $m∠A=m∠B,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠B \\\\ 8w-{2}^{∘} & =9w-{10}^{∘} \\\\ w & ={8}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, substituting $w={8}^{∘}$ in the expression for $m∠A,$ we get\n\n$$\nm∠A=8({8}^{∘})-{2}^{∘}={62}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find $m∠C$ using the fact that the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠A+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\cdot {62}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {124}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={56}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488060 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488253 1069 "q-93821" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488253 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488253 1072 #uuid "b54ddcc0-5401-5d92-820d-ec32389dd88e" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488253 1074 "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-2.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488143 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488254 1069 "q-178560" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488254 1071 "Given that $\\frac{h}{8}=k,$ express $h$ in terms of $k.$\n\n$h=${{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488254 1072 #uuid "b63796bb-cd51-5db1-b65b-8d15ce564d53" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488254 1074 "We need to isolate the variable $h.$ To do this, we treat $k$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nIn this case, we simply multiply both sides of the equation by $8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{h}{8} & =k \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{h}{8} & =8\\cdot k \\\\ \\cancel{8}\\cdot \\frac{h}{\\cancel{8}} & =8k \\\\ h & =8k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488165 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488255 1069 "q-171603" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488255 1071 "If $-\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{4y+3}{5},$ then $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488255 1072 #uuid "bb61cd26-be58-5f6a-a7c8-fd462a5c81f3" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488255 1074 "We transfer the negative into the numerator of the fraction, and proceed with cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y+5}{3} & =\\frac{4y+3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-(y+5)}{3} & =\\frac{4y+3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-y-5}{3} & =\\frac{4y+3}{5} \\\\ (-y-5)\\cdot 5 & =3\\cdot (4y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-y-5)\\cdot 5 & =3\\cdot (4y+3) \\\\ -5y-25 & =12y+9 \\\\ -5y-25+5y & =12y+9+5y \\\\ -25 & =17y+9 \\\\ -25-9 & =17y+9-9 \\\\ -34 & =17y \\\\ \\frac{-34}{17} & =\\frac{17y}{17} \\\\ -2 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $y=-2.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488089 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488256 1069 "q-225061" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488256 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(5<X<9).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ | $\\,0.7166\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488256 1072 #uuid "bfd17aa5-30fb-5cb9-aef8-99d3d322cf8d" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488256 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(8) & =P(X<9) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,8\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\})+P(X\\in \\{6,7,8\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 5)+P(5<X<9) \\\\  & =F(5)+P(5<X<9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<9) & =F(8)-F(5) \\\\  & =0.5925-0.1912 \\\\  & =0.4013.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488117 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488257 1069 "q-101686" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488257 1071 "Solve for $q$ where $\\frac{3-2q}{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488257 1072 #uuid "c11f4c73-9d61-5e53-addf-f63464a71f44" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488257 1074 "We transfer the negative into the numerator of the fraction, and proceed with cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-2q}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ (3-2q)\\cdot 5 & =3\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-2q)\\cdot 5 & =3\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 15-10q & =-3 \\\\ 15-10q-15 & =-3-15 \\\\ -10q & =-18 \\\\ \\frac{-10q}{-10} & =\\frac{-18}{-10} \\\\ q & =\\frac{18}{10} \\\\ q & =\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488085 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488258 1069 "q-37027" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488258 1071 "What is the range of the function $h$ defined by the following table?\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $4$ | $-5$ | $6$ | $-7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $h(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $5$ | $-6$ | $7$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488258 1072 #uuid "c17be40c-81be-54ae-93a6-293eae7f05d7" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488258 1074 "The range of $h(x)$ consists of all the outputs that have a corresponding $x$-input.\n\nIn a table, the range consists of all numbers that feature in the row corresponding to $h(x).$\n\nTherefore, the range is the set $\\{-6,-4,-2,0,1,3,5,7\\}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488144 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488259 1069 "q-44339" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488259 1071 "Solve for $k$ where $\\frac{9k-5}{6}=\\frac{k}{2}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488259 1072 #uuid "c21712e7-21c4-5b84-b421-b21aa39ca232" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488259 1074 "First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9k-5}{6} & =\\frac{k}{2} \\\\ (9k-5)\\cdot 2 & =6\\cdot k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9k-5)\\cdot 2 & =6\\cdot k \\\\ 18k-10 & =6k \\\\ 18k-10-18k & =6k-18k \\\\ -10 & =-12k \\\\ \\frac{-10}{-12} & =\\frac{-12k}{-12} \\\\ \\frac{10}{12} & =k \\\\ \\frac{5}{6} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=\\frac{5}{6}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488175 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488260 1069 "q-223145" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488260 1071 "If ${4}^{x}=\\frac{1}{4},$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488260 1072 #uuid "c5891ca3-f381-532b-913f-b6dc3bcab055" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488260 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{4}$ as ${4}^{-1}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & ={4}^{-1} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488092 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488261 1069 "q-111930" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488261 1071 "If $f(t)=A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}$ and $f(3)=4,$ then $A=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488261 1072 #uuid "c97061a8-e888-5c8e-ae09-c8d25cf5d2a1" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488261 1074 "We substitute $t=3$ and $f(t)=4$ into the expression for $f(t)$ and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\ 4 & =A\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\ 4\\cdot 125 & =A \\\\ 500 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=500.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488155 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488262 1069 "q-81214" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488262 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $H$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488262 1072 #uuid "cc5327fa-e153-5237-b670-7841684eaae5" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488262 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $H,$ then $H$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $H=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $H,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nH={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 21 \\\\ 4 & -14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 \\\\ -10 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488163 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488263 1069 "q-164375" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488263 1071 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperboloid of two sheets $-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-1?$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488263 1072 #uuid "cfd858a6-a00f-5a94-8347-c41608b62519" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488263 1074 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperboloid. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of a hyperbola in $xz$-plane." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488129 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488264 1069 "q-253911" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488264 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n}\\cdot (n-1)\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488264 1072 #uuid "d021d0f6-fbf3-5eed-804d-eee551071be6" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488264 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot (n-1),\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/58/5812e3dd22994d613ecc84087418cddfa978e98c1324869f290e1bf2b1317bad.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating, non-convergent sequence. Moreover, $A$ is unbounded above. In other words, for any real number $x,$ we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ such that ${a}_{n}$ is greater than $x.$ Therefore, $A$ has no upper bounds and $sup(A)=+\\infty .$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488133 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488265 1069 "q-77697" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488265 1071 "If $y(t)=A\\cdot {5}^{t}$ and $y(2)=175,$ then $A=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488265 1072 #uuid "d094ad0f-c26b-57d4-8a3d-3a131e61b0c8" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488265 1074 "We substitute $t=2$ and $y(t)=175$ into the expression for $y(t)$ and then solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}175 & =A\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{175}{{5}^{2}} & =A \\\\ \\frac{175}{25} & =A \\\\ 7 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=7.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488157 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488266 1069 "q-225056" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488266 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(3)$ given below, compute $P(1\\le X\\le 4).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0498\\,$ | $\\,0.1991\\,$ | $\\,0.4232\\,$ | $\\,0.6472\\,$ | $\\,0.8153\\,$ | $\\,0.9161\\,$ | $\\,0.9665\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488266 1072 #uuid "d0dd1d6e-e305-5637-930e-c61c5f8b750e" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488266 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0\\})+P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 0)+P(1\\le X\\le 4) \\\\  & =F(0)+P(1\\le X\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 4) & =F(4)-F(0) \\\\  & =0.8153-0.0498 \\\\  & =0.7655.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488159 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488267 1069 "q-45310" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488267 1071 "If $4c+5=3yc-2,$ then $c=$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488267 1072 #uuid "d1bde4eb-16c8-5f7c-8125-caf4a9cb1949" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488267 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $c$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $5$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c+5 & =3yc-2 \\\\ 4c+5-5 & =3yc-2-5 \\\\ 4c & =3yc-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3yc$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c & =3yc-7 \\\\ 4c-3yc & =3yc-7-3yc \\\\ 4c-3yc & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $c$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $c$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $c$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c-3yc & =-7 \\\\ c(4-3y) & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(4-3y)$ to isolate $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(4-3y) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{c(4-3y)}{4-3y} & =\\frac{-7}{4-3y} \\\\ \\frac{c\\cancel{(4-3y)}}{\\cancel{4-3y}} & =-\\frac{7}{4-3y} \\\\ c & =\\frac{7}{3y-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3y-4\\ne 0.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488051 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488065 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488101 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488107 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1069 "q-253553" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1071 "Consider the set $B=[-1,2).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1072 #uuid "d48b0cc5-05e8-55c3-b441-64611554a8ed" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488268 1074 "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-1]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is also the minimum of $B.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le -1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(B)=-1\\in B.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488154 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488269 1069 "q-129499" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488269 1071 "![](images/38/3812ff158f2268f78bbc22cc0fa11f86fd8506a78ce05104df04fef1fe39d037.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488269 1072 #uuid "d9cb8bb3-9f8a-56a1-975b-7665fdd7f9a0" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488269 1074 "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (-4,-2)$ is $(-2,1)$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [2,4)$ is $[2,4)$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $(-2,1)\\cup [2,4).$\n\n**Note:** From the graph, we see that the values $y=-2,$ $y=1$ and $y=4$ are NOT valid outputs of the function $y=f(x).$ Therefore, we use parentheses $(…)$ instead of brackets $[…]$ at these endpoints." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488078 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488270 1069 "q-98695" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488270 1071 "![](images/23/2304f9b9080a8b3ec4ff10284cee8e01ec8dff54fb879f7ed55dd7a3f80e2b23.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488270 1072 #uuid "db6c8509-7f18-5c65-821d-f6d09e6d4e13" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488270 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only.\"" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488076 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488271 1069 "q-253895" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488271 1071 "![](images/50/50b52da574101dfca7c620411a446f2c5f3ef41242186ca38da072e140019024.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488271 1072 #uuid "ddf4ec2c-4f4d-56c1-bc14-78cfd2f52940" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488271 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-2,3].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 3$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<3$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $3,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[3,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[3,\\infty )=3.\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488140 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488272 1069 "q-235484" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488272 1071 "![](images/59/5948fac552a311cfac7023171d07b41261fade92d026a747f87d3865d22e9307.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488272 1072 #uuid "de5674f7-089c-5512-bf55-3b781441e00f" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488272 1074 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles in any triangle is equal to ${180}^{∘}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{47}^{∘}+{58}^{∘}+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {105}^{∘}+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={75}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488156 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488273 1069 "q-37623" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488273 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488273 1072 #uuid "deae4223-84c9-5248-9dcf-afbc4aa0a08d" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488273 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 24 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 12 \\\\ -30 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488122 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488274 1069 "q-140130" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488274 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488274 1072 #uuid "e138229b-152f-5c0e-9e47-2d76a5b09b43" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488274 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A$, then  $A$ must be diagonalizable. \n\nSo, we can write $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $A$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488053 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488058 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488062 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488275 1069 "q-236460" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488275 1071 "![](images/6a/6a3822492b4d8fda25ad48a7edd44cf82209857f7a1d67997ff691a770264afb.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488275 1072 #uuid "e22474d8-8407-520c-b16d-2ce97d2431d2" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488275 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- There is no smallest $y$-value.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $3.$ This occurs when $x=0.$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-\\infty $ and $3,$ including $3.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-\\infty <y\\le 3,$ which can also be written as  $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 3.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488067 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488149 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488276 1069 "q-224984" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488276 1071 "Consider the Poisson distribution $\\text{Po}(6).$ Rounding to $4$ decimal digits, fill in the missing values in the corresponding table for the cumulative distribution function below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $\\,0.0620\\,$ | $\\,0.1512\\,$ | $\\,0.2851\\,$ | $\\,0.4457\\,$ | $\\,0.6063\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488276 1072 #uuid "e7faa58d-c8b1-553c-876f-43b3b6090485" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488276 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function for the Poisson distribution $\\text{Po}(\\lambda )$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,3,…\\,.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $\\lambda =6.$ \n\nTherefore, we compute the missing elements of the table as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =P(X\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(X=0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{0}{e}^{-6}}{0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-6}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.0025\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & =P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{0}\\cdot {e}^{-6}}{0!}+\\frac{{6}^{1}\\cdot {e}^{-6}}{1!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-6}}{1}+\\frac{6\\cdot {e}^{-6}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.0174\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488112 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488277 1069 "q-101672" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488277 1071 "Solve for $k$ where $\\frac{5k+2}{3}=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488277 1072 #uuid "ea7a15b7-4301-5282-88af-359487b5befc" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488277 1074 "First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5k+2}{3} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ (5k+2)\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot (3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5k+2)\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot (3) \\\\ 10k+4 & =9 \\\\ 10k+4-4 & =9-4 \\\\ 10k & =5 \\\\ \\frac{10k}{10} & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488099 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488278 1069 "q-129337" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488278 1071 "![](images/d9/d94225e60a13742a70558cea252d7bffde2bab51bcb6942be510e7bbde6323e5.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488278 1072 #uuid "f18ce5af-43bd-5993-814d-ed1e89769ace" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488278 1074 "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs at the point $(4,0).$ However, note that this point is not actually included in the graph.\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $3.$ This occurs at the point $(-2,3).$ However, note that this point is not actually included in the graph.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $0$ and $3,$ but not including $0$ nor $3.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $0<y<3,$ which can also be written as  $0<f(x)<3.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488118 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488279 1069 "q-111695" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488279 1071 "Solve the equation ${7}^{-3}={7}^{y}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488279 1072 #uuid "f1e12ec8-496f-581a-aa45-273ef2ee6953" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488279 1074 "Since both sides of the equation share the same base $(7)$, we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{-3} & ={7}^{y} \\\\ -3 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=-3.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488125 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488280 1069 "q-218659" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488280 1071 "Given that $h(t)=5\\cdot {4}^{3t+6},$ find $t$ such that $h(t)=320.$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488280 1072 #uuid "f6033ad1-0d99-515b-be18-2f73585eb731" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488280 1074 "First, we substitute $h(t)=320$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot {4}^{3t+6}=320\n$$\n\nWe can divide both sides of the equation by the constant factor of $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\cdot {4}^{3t+6}}{5} & =\\frac{320}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot {4}^{3t+6}}{\\cancel{5}} & =64 \\\\ {4}^{3t+6} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $t$ using the fact that $64$ is a power of $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3t+6} & ={4}^{3} \\\\ 3t+6 & =3 \\\\ 3t & =-3 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488174 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488281 1069 "q-79266" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488281 1071 "What is the range of the function $f$ defined by the following table?\n\n| $x$ | $6$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ | $14$ | $18$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $0$ | $8$ | $-10$ | $11$ | $-10$ | $5$ | $0$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488281 1072 #uuid "f6271452-0c9d-5cbb-808a-a03630c0e389" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488281 1074 "The range of $f(x)$ consists of all the outputs that have a corresponding $x$-input.\n\nIn a table, the range consists of all numbers that feature in the row corresponding to $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the range is the set $\\{-10,0,5,8,11\\}.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488071 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488282 1069 "q-140358" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488282 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488282 1072 #uuid "f89a34e2-edc3-5894-bae7-80f53ccb3dc4" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488282 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488164 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488283 1069 "q-224965" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488283 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(4)$ given below, compute $P(3<X\\le 7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.2381\\,$ | $\\,0.4335\\,$ | $\\,0.6288\\,$ | $\\,0.7851\\,$ | $\\,0.8893\\,$ | $\\,0.9489\\,$ | $\\,0.9786\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488283 1072 #uuid "f97c8702-16dc-518f-875a-9b5c78750b8c" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488283 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(7) & =P(X\\le 7) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,7\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\})+P(X\\in \\{4,5,6,7\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 3)+P(3<X\\le 7) \\\\  & =F(3)+P(3<X\\le 7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X\\le 7) & =F(7)-F(3) \\\\  & =0.9489-0.4335 \\\\  & =0.5154.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488086 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488284 1069 "q-223149" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488284 1071 "If ${6}^{t}=\\frac{1}{36},$ then $t=${{field-1}}." 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488284 1072 #uuid "fa2ae142-bf31-5242-a307-d8faa66809d6" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488284 1074 "First, we rewrite $36$ as ${6}^{2}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{t} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {6}^{t} & =\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}} \\\\ {6}^{t} & ={6}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{t} & ={6}^{-2} \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t=-2.$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488131 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488285 1069 "q-225083" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488285 1071 "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(7)$ given below, compute $P(X>9).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.3007\\,$ | $\\,0.4497\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.8305\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ | $\\,0.9467\\,$ |\n\n$P(X>9)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488285 1072 #uuid "fcbdd763-0262-5c2f-847d-f89b9e62dad1" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488285 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>9) & =1-P(X\\le 9) \\\\  & =1-F(9) \\\\  & =1-0.8305 \\\\  & =0.1695.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488054 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488120 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488286 1069 "q-224988" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488286 1071 "Consider the Poisson distribution $\\text{Po}(7).$ Rounding to $4$ decimal digits, fill in the missing values in the corresponding table for the cumulative distribution function below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $\\,0.0296\\,$ | $\\,0.0818\\,$ | $\\,0.1730\\,$ | $\\,0.3007\\,$ | $\\,0.4497\\,$ |" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488286 1072 #uuid "fef70086-7b53-5958-83a3-40bb6490de70" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488286 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function for the Poisson distribution $\\text{Po}(\\lambda )$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,3,…\\,.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nTherefore, we compute the missing elements of the table as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =P(X\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(X=0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}{e}^{-7}}{0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-7}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.0009\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & =P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{0}\\cdot {e}^{-7}}{0!}+\\frac{{7}^{1}\\cdot {e}^{-7}}{1!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{-7}}{1}+\\frac{7\\cdot {e}^{-7}}{1} \\\\  & ≈0.0073\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488287 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488326 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488287 1062 #uuid "0320c674-391c-525d-a5c4-9c442fc33f7e" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488287 1063 #edb/ref 17592186488281 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488288 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488287 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488288 1062 #uuid "062e6706-d792-58b5-acc0-8902fc891e0b" 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488288 1063 #edb/ref 17592186488258 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488289 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488328 13194139535264 17592186045417 true] [17592186488289 1062 #uuid "07f5fb55-